INT. FARMHOUSE KITCHEN - PRE-DAWN

A bare bulb. A pot of coffee going cold. A calendar with a date circled in red.

EDMUND ABERNETHY-SOTO, 60s, sits at the table with a stack of unopened envelopes. His daughter, LIORA ABERNETHY-SOTO, 30s, stands at the sink with her back to him. A hired hand, QUENTIN DUPREE, 40s, hovers by the door, cap in his hands.

He lifts one envelope. Sets it down. Lifts another.

She runs the tap. Turns it off. Runs it again.

QUENTIN
Mr. Abernethy, the auctioneer's truck is at the gate.

EDMUND
Already?

QUENTIN
Six a.m., he said.

LIORA
It's four-thirty.

QUENTIN
They like to get a good spot.

He opens an envelope. Reads. Closes it. She turns, wipes her hands on her apron, sits across from him. Neither speaks.

EDMUND
Your mother loved this kitchen.

LIORA
She hated the stove.

EDMUND
She hated the stove, yes.

QUENTIN
(awkward)
I'll start with the tractor.

LIORA
Quentin, wait.

QUENTIN
Ma'am?

EDMUND
Let him go, Liora.

LIORA
He doesn't have to.

EDMUND
It's his job.

QUENTIN
It's all right.

EDMUND
Sit down, son.

QUENTIN
I'd rather stand.

He stands. They sit. The kitchen clock ticks. Outside, an engine idles. A truck door slams.

He pushes the stack of envelopes to the middle of the table. She takes one. Opens it. Reads. Her mouth tightens. She folds it small.

She stands and fills the kettle.
